Ledger customers were affected by unauthorized access to a cloud-based information system operated by Global-e, a third-party e-commerce provider supporting Ledger purchases. The compromise exposed customer identity, contact, shipping, and order information, including names, postal addresses, email addresses, phone numbers, products purchased, and pricing information. Ledger’s hardware wallets, recovery phrases, private keys, wallet balances, software, and payment information were not compromised. The deeper security concern was therefore not direct wallet compromise but the creation of a highly contextual dataset identifying real people as owners or purchasers of cryptocurrency security products. That information can materially improve phishing, impersonation, social-engineering, and potentially physical targeting attempts. The incident demonstrates that the security boundary around a security product extends into the commercial systems that identify who owns it.
